      "durationSec": 4.504
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "template": "compare",
      "narration": "The old way makes an empty list, then loops and appends to it. A comprehension does all of that in a single expression.",
      "text": {
        "kicker": "Before vs after",
        "heading": "Same result, less noise",
        "compare": {
          "divider": "VS",
          "left": {
            "label": "for loop",
            "tone": "bad",
            "items": ["make an empty list", "loop, then append", "three lines"]
          },
          "right": {
            "label": "comprehension",
            "tone": "good",
            "items": ["build it inline", "one expression", "one line"]
          }
        }
      },
      "durationSec": 6.567
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "template": "code",
      "narration": "The shape is simple: an expression, then a for clause. This squares every number from one to five.",
      "sfx": [
        { "cue": "pop", "offset": 0.5 },
        { "cue": "ding", "offset": 3.2 }
      ],
      "text": {
        "heading": "The one-liner",
        "windowLabel": "squares.py",
        "footnote": "[ expression for item in iterable ]",
        "code": ["squares = [n * n for n in range(1, 6)]"],
        "output": ["[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]"],
        "highlights": [{ "line": 1, "start": 1.4 }]
      },
      "durationSec": 5.963
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "template": "code",
      "narration": "Add an if at the end to filter. Here we keep only the even numbers under ten.",
      "sfx": [
        { "cue": "pop", "offset": 0.5 },
        { "cue": "ding", "offset": 3.0 }
      ],
      "text": {
        "heading": "Add a filter",
        "windowLabel": "evens.py",
        "footnote": "a trailing if keeps only what you want",
        "code": ["evens = [", "  n for n in range(10)", "  if n % 2 == 0", "]"],
        "output": ["[0, 2, 4, 6, 8]"],
        "highlights": [{ "line": 3, "start": 2.0, "color": "#FFD23F" }]
      },
      "durationSec": 4.466
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "template": "bullets",
      "narration": "Reach for it on simple transforms. It's clean, and usually faster than a manual loop. But when the logic gets nested, a normal loop is kinder to read.",
      "sfx": [
        { "cue": "click", "offset": 0.4 },
        { "cue": "click", "offset": 2.2 },
        { "cue": "click", "offset": 4.0 }
      ],
      "text": {
        "kicker": "Rule of thumb",
        "heading": "When to use it",
        "bullets": [
          "great for simple transforms",
          "usually faster than a loop",
          "skip it when logic gets nested"
        ]
      },
      "durationSec": 9.204
